WEBVTT

00:00.160 --> 00:03.512
Thank you. Regional Chair Redman? Present. Councillor Craig?

00:03.576 --> 00:06.792
Present. Councillor Erb? Present. Councillor Foxton.

00:06.856 --> 00:10.280
Present. Chair Harris? Present. Councillor Huinink.

00:10.440 --> 00:14.392
Present. Councillor James. Present. Councillor Liggett?

00:14.536 --> 00:19.224
Present. Councillor McCabe? Present. Councillor Nowak.

00:19.352 --> 00:21.688
Yes. Councillor Salonen.

00:21.864 --> 00:26.808
Present. Councillor Schantz. Present. Councillor Vrbanovic.

00:26.984 --> 00:32.376
Present. Councillor Williams sends

00:32.408 --> 00:35.288
regrets, I think. Yes. And Councillor Wolf.

00:35.384 --> 00:38.872
Present. I would

00:38.896 --> 00:42.616
like to acknowledge that the land on which we gather today is the traditional territory

00:42.648 --> 00:46.376
of the Haudenosaunee, Anishinaabe and Chonnonton peoples. We acknowledge

00:46.408 --> 00:49.480
the enduring presence of the Indigenous people with whom we share this land today,

00:49.520 --> 00:53.140
their achievements and their contributions, of course, to our community. Are there any

00:53.180 --> 00:56.324
declarations of pecuniary interest? Seeing none,

00:56.372 --> 00:59.540
there are no presentations. We will now proceed with delegations as

00:59.580 --> 01:02.980
listed on the agenda. I'll call

01:03.020 --> 01:05.640
upon Peter Woolstencroft.

01:06.860 --> 01:11.156
Peter, I believe you're online. Good afternoon. Good hearing

01:11.188 --> 01:13.000
from you. You've got five minutes.

01:14.300 --> 01:33.838
If you're well,

01:33.894 --> 01:37.774
we can come back to. Peter, I think we've got Scott Piatkowski.

01:37.822 --> 01:42.450
Actually, no, it's Brendan Sherratt. Charat Sharat.

01:43.510 --> 01:45.770
Brendan. Pardon me.

01:47.430 --> 01:51.070
Oh, yeah. Actually, we'll go to Scott Piatkowski.

01:51.150 --> 01:54.238
Scott, I know you're online there, so good afternoon.

01:54.334 --> 01:55.650
You've got five minutes.

01:57.910 --> 02:00.398
Good afternoon, regional councillors,

02:00.494 --> 02:03.934
staff, fellow citizens of the region of Waterloo

02:03.982 --> 02:07.790
who may be watching. Not sure how many fans you have,

02:07.830 --> 02:11.192
but first, in case there was

02:11.216 --> 02:14.920
any doubt, I wanted to clarify that I am speaking to you today as

02:14.960 --> 02:18.600
an individual. My comments are not meant to be representative of any

02:18.640 --> 02:22.200
organization of which I'm a member and should not be taken as

02:22.240 --> 02:25.752
such. As someone who has been a

02:25.776 --> 02:28.792
candidate in seven election campaigns and been

02:28.816 --> 02:32.072
involved in several dozen other campaigns, I wanted to take

02:32.096 --> 02:36.130
the opportunity to express my support for the region's existing

02:36.210 --> 02:39.794
sign bylaw as it pertains to banning election signs

02:39.842 --> 02:43.990
along regional roads. Under the old bylaw,

02:44.410 --> 02:48.450
candidates had to post signs on public property to be seen as

02:48.490 --> 02:52.070
competitive. This was wasteful and expensive,

02:52.490 --> 02:55.570
and it represented a significant financial barrier

02:55.650 --> 02:59.794
for candidates wanting to enter the race who may have had limited

02:59.922 --> 03:03.510
financial resources or fundraising capacity.

03:04.610 --> 03:08.310
Signs along public roadways aren't really

03:08.690 --> 03:11.834
an indication of support. Rather,

03:11.882 --> 03:15.274
they are an indication of the financial resources that candidates

03:15.322 --> 03:19.210
have. It's better to limit signage to

03:19.250 --> 03:23.418
that which is requested by property owners or on

03:23.474 --> 03:25.870
lawns, balconies or windows.

03:26.850 --> 03:29.946
Getting permission to post signs there requires real work,

03:30.018 --> 03:35.214
and more importantly, it requires actual signs. Support posting

03:35.262 --> 03:38.542
and later, collecting signs along busy regional roads

03:38.606 --> 03:41.806
means stopping on those roads, sometimes in bike lanes,

03:41.918 --> 03:45.710
sometimes in Car lanes. In many cases, it means stopping where

03:45.750 --> 03:48.974
stopping is legally prohibited. This creates

03:49.022 --> 03:52.766
a hazard for those posting the signs for other drivers

03:52.878 --> 03:56.930
and for other more vulnerable road users.

03:57.430 --> 04:01.326
Think of the kinds of roads in question, like Weber Street, Homer Watson

04:01.358 --> 04:04.346
Boulevard, Ottawa Street, Franklin Boulevard,

04:04.458 --> 04:07.658
University Avenue, Old Highway 8, Erb street,

04:07.714 --> 04:10.922
or Fisher Home and Road. These are simply not safe

04:10.986 --> 04:14.750
places for stopping to put up or take down signs.

04:15.490 --> 04:19.498
For candidates running region wide, say for regional chair or

04:19.554 --> 04:23.950
citywide for mayor, regional council or school trustee,

04:24.450 --> 04:27.914
it's difficult to collect all of what our friend Mike

04:27.962 --> 04:31.978
Farwell likes to call your ding dang signs within

04:32.034 --> 04:34.750
the 72 hour window that is allowed.

04:35.250 --> 04:39.002
Signs posted on public land are more likely to end up thrown in

04:39.026 --> 04:42.682
a ditch or flattened by mowers, making them more difficult to

04:42.706 --> 04:46.762
pick up. Inevitably, some get missed. Some candidates

04:46.826 --> 04:50.218
just don't bother. Election signs that are

04:50.274 --> 04:54.010
too ubiquitous become nothing but clutter. And we

04:54.050 --> 04:57.390
certainly don't need more of that in our public spaces.

04:58.130 --> 05:02.730
Let's continue to ban all election signs from regional roadways.

05:02.890 --> 05:06.666
It is fairer for candidates, it minimizes roadside

05:06.778 --> 05:09.670
distractions, and it's better for the environment.

05:16.770 --> 05:19.990
Well, thank you, Scott. I don't have any questions. Appreciate your time,

05:20.370 --> 05:23.722
Peter. We'll go back to you if you're with us.

05:23.746 --> 05:24.430
Peter,

05:33.180 --> 05:34.240
Is he coming?

05:47.340 --> 05:50.840
Can you hear me now? Hey, Peter, we got you now. Okay,

05:52.370 --> 05:55.070
thank you. And I'll be very brief.

05:55.650 --> 05:58.986
I want to second what Scott Piatkowski just said.

05:59.138 --> 06:03.098
I'm. I'm a political scientist. I taught a lot of courses on party selection

06:03.194 --> 06:06.270
voting. This is very much in my wheelhouse.

06:07.090 --> 06:10.714
I've been involved in over 40 elections. I've been putting up signs for a

06:10.722 --> 06:14.630
long time. I avoided the last election. Now to the points.

06:15.170 --> 06:18.430
This, on this motion, I,

06:18.470 --> 06:22.302
I point out that some people say, well, if we had signs, we'd have

06:22.326 --> 06:26.206
higher turnout this last election. In the five

06:26.278 --> 06:30.078
ridings in Waterloo Region, their turnout in

06:30.134 --> 06:33.486
four of those ridings is higher than the turnout across

06:33.558 --> 06:37.054
the province of Ontario. Only in one of

06:37.062 --> 06:41.374
the writings, Kitchener center was the turnout a little bit under the provincial

06:41.422 --> 06:45.224
average. So it cannot be argued that the absence of signs

06:45.272 --> 06:49.912
was a problematic. In terms of the turnout on

06:49.936 --> 06:53.384
the second point, I would argue that

06:53.472 --> 06:57.736
the absence of signs along arterial roads is

06:57.808 --> 07:01.944
good for challengers because all candidates

07:01.992 --> 07:07.400
are forced to go into the neighborhoods and meet people where

07:07.440 --> 07:11.690
they are. Rather than relying upon the tried old method

07:11.850 --> 07:15.690
of putting expensive signs along arterial roads

07:15.850 --> 07:19.050
and relying upon the well

07:19.090 --> 07:22.874
established proposition in political science that in municipal

07:22.922 --> 07:26.842
elections the biggest predictor for success in

07:26.866 --> 07:31.050
an election is whether you won in the last election. So incumbency

07:31.130 --> 07:35.082
matters. And incumbents have big signs and they know place them

07:35.106 --> 07:38.056
along the roads and people can see the sign say, oh,

07:38.088 --> 07:41.592
there's, there's Susan and there's Billy and so on. I know

07:41.616 --> 07:44.888
them and I go and vote for them. And what we want

07:44.944 --> 07:48.552
is to have challengers and incumbents

07:48.696 --> 07:52.680
competing in the neighborhoods for votes of people who

07:52.720 --> 07:56.296
are going to vote in the election. And so I think it's great,

07:56.368 --> 07:59.320
actually we do not have signs on arterial roads.

07:59.400 --> 08:03.330
It changes the whole electoral marketplace and

08:03.370 --> 08:08.594
makes it a much more competitive and more democratic context.

08:08.722 --> 08:12.066
And that's one of the reasons why I think there have been many

08:12.218 --> 08:15.810
new people elected to city councils and

08:15.850 --> 08:18.830
regional councils in the last go round.

08:19.450 --> 08:23.506
So in sum, I'm fully in support of maintaining the status

08:23.538 --> 08:27.106
quo. Thank you. Okay.

08:27.138 --> 08:30.872
Peter, thanks so much. I know we've got Brent. Councillor Craig

08:30.936 --> 08:33.980
oh, I'm sorry, I didn't have it on the screen.

08:34.480 --> 08:37.928
Councillor Craig Peter,

08:37.944 --> 08:41.240
I appreciate your commentary. I'm interested in

08:41.280 --> 08:45.416
your thoughts on the diminishing attendance at elections

08:45.528 --> 08:49.976
for municipal councillors. What do you think would

08:50.048 --> 08:53.980
enhance that? What do you think the reason is that people aren't involved?

08:56.330 --> 08:59.874
Councillor Craig we have a big problem in the liberal democracies. The decline

08:59.922 --> 09:03.698
of turnout and particularly manifest

09:03.794 --> 09:07.474
in municipal elections. We have in various parts of

09:07.482 --> 09:11.554
Ontario too many acclimations. So the declining interest

09:11.642 --> 09:13.470
is a problem. I'm a big,

09:14.890 --> 09:18.306
I'm very much of the view that the problem is

09:18.458 --> 09:21.746
that our school systems do not stress the value

09:21.818 --> 09:25.400
of being a citizen. And a citizen means responsibilities

09:25.480 --> 09:29.272
and means rights. And I say to anybody

09:29.336 --> 09:32.552
who will listen to me on this that we should value living in

09:32.576 --> 09:36.008
a democracy because it's a very different kind of place than living in a non

09:36.064 --> 09:39.592
democratic system. And if you believe in the concept and the

09:39.616 --> 09:43.192
value of being a citizen, then that means you will pay more attention

09:43.256 --> 09:47.320
to elections whether they be federal, provincial or municipal.

09:47.400 --> 09:51.240
So I lament the decline in turnout last

09:51.280 --> 09:54.504
election. I thought it was kind of sad that we got excited. We went

09:54.512 --> 09:58.200
from 44 to 45% in the province of Ontario.

09:58.360 --> 10:01.896
That's, that's, that is a problem. But it's, it has nothing

10:01.928 --> 10:04.984
to do with the technicalities of running an election in,

10:05.072 --> 10:08.920
in, in Waterloo or anywhere else. It has to do with the

10:08.960 --> 10:12.376
decline of interest in the common good.

10:12.528 --> 10:16.746
And that comes from understanding that you are a citizen and you

10:16.818 --> 10:20.282
as a citizen have the right and the obligation to participate in the

10:20.306 --> 10:23.466
political process. If I may, Mr. Chair,

10:23.538 --> 10:26.858
just a follow up question. Peter I'm glad

10:26.874 --> 10:30.250
we got you on the line. What are your

10:30.290 --> 10:33.802
thoughts about in some areas of the

10:33.826 --> 10:38.074
world, as example, I think Scotland, where they lowered the voting

10:38.122 --> 10:41.892
age on a referendum, do you think we should be looking at

10:42.026 --> 10:45.728
perhaps lowering the voting age to get people more young

10:45.784 --> 10:49.232
people more involved? I just. I would be interested in what your thoughts on that

10:49.256 --> 10:52.896
are. I think if you lower the age,

10:52.968 --> 10:56.480
you would lower the overall turnout rate

10:56.520 --> 11:00.352
in the election. And now if

11:00.376 --> 11:04.688
you had a well structured citizenship

11:04.864 --> 11:08.700
course in high school, you might have a different result.

11:10.100 --> 11:13.740
I think the question really is the matter that we

11:13.780 --> 11:17.500
do not put a premium on being a citizen. If we lower the

11:17.540 --> 11:20.668
age, some people will vote, but a lot of people will not vote.

11:20.764 --> 11:24.876
We'll find out when my political science colleagues do a study recent

11:24.948 --> 11:28.956
election whether there was an increase in youth turnout.

11:29.068 --> 11:32.348
I think there was a little bit in Waterloo because the turnout went up about

11:32.404 --> 11:35.760
4%. But that's university and college students who were

11:35.800 --> 11:39.312
here this time around rather than the last election which was

11:39.336 --> 11:42.864
in June three years ago. An interesting

11:42.912 --> 11:46.512
question. I don't have hard views one way or another. I think,

11:46.616 --> 11:50.000
however, I will stress the importance of being a

11:50.040 --> 11:53.088
citizen and our school systems fail to address

11:53.144 --> 11:57.140
that. Totally agree with you. Thank you, sir. Chair Redman,

11:59.000 --> 12:02.348
thank you very much, Chair Harris and Peter,

12:02.364 --> 12:06.080
it's wonderful of somebody of your stature and your knowledge.

12:06.420 --> 12:09.996
So I think you got it right. I really agree with your take

12:10.068 --> 12:14.140
on regional roads and whether or not there should be

12:14.260 --> 12:17.820
political signage during campaigns. I'm wondering just

12:17.860 --> 12:21.612
along sort of the line that Councillor Craig was talking. I know there have

12:21.636 --> 12:25.420
been international studies where voters needed to register and

12:25.460 --> 12:28.988
where voters. I think it's in Australia or New Zealand where they are

12:29.044 --> 12:32.556
fined if they don't vote. Have you studied that in your expansive

12:32.588 --> 12:36.504
career? And do you have a view on how successful that is in promoting

12:36.552 --> 12:39.176
voter turnout? Well,

12:39.248 --> 12:43.048
mandatory voting is a very important question

12:43.104 --> 12:46.600
and yes, I have studied it widely. I am a supporter of

12:46.640 --> 12:50.824
mandatory voting and Australia is the prime example

12:50.872 --> 12:55.000
of that. And people object, say we get all kinds of people voting

12:55.160 --> 12:59.112
who are not paying any attention and they're just voting not to

12:59.216 --> 13:02.822
avoid the fine. However, the evidence as I see

13:02.846 --> 13:06.246
it in Australia is that when people are required to vote,

13:06.278 --> 13:09.862
they actually pay attention to what's going on. They're better informed than

13:09.886 --> 13:13.350
their counterparts in systems where you're not required to vote.

13:13.430 --> 13:16.646
You know, if you have to buy a bicycle, then you spend some time looking

13:16.718 --> 13:20.598
at what bicycles are all about and you make your best choice.

13:20.774 --> 13:24.770
So that. I think that's an interesting Analogy. A fair analogy to your question.

13:25.880 --> 13:29.472
Thank you, Councillor Huinink. Thank you,

13:29.496 --> 13:33.580
Chair Harris. And thank you, Mr. Woolstencroft, for sharing your expertise with us today.

13:33.880 --> 13:38.128
I, I, I appreciate your comments and I wholeheartedly

13:38.144 --> 13:41.040
agree with you. I'm wondering your perspective on.

13:41.160 --> 13:44.480
I know that regional clerks commonly go into

13:44.520 --> 13:48.096
grade five classrooms. Do you think it would be beneficial

13:48.208 --> 13:51.440
for them to also make a habit of going into post

13:51.480 --> 13:55.570
secondary institutions and even grade 11 and 12 classrooms

13:55.650 --> 13:59.698
to further elaborate on the differences between

13:59.834 --> 14:02.514
regional and municipal governments?

14:02.642 --> 14:06.242
Specifically, yes.

14:06.306 --> 14:10.018
I'm an educator. I believe in education. I think we

14:10.154 --> 14:13.910
need to increase the knowledge base

14:14.810 --> 14:18.290
of what people about and talk

14:18.330 --> 14:21.970
about. I listen to a lot of people and I'm astonished at their

14:22.010 --> 14:25.992
lack of knowledge about things. I despair about it. And the only way we

14:26.016 --> 14:29.900
can address it is by having programs in our schools

14:30.240 --> 14:34.200
which, which, which get young people aware

14:34.360 --> 14:38.104
of. I'm repeating myself here, being a citizen. And I noticed in the

14:38.112 --> 14:40.520
city of Waterloo they have a mayor per day,

14:40.640 --> 14:44.184
calstrs for day a day. That's an excellent program because it

14:44.192 --> 14:48.104
makes people aware about the human side of government and politics

14:48.232 --> 14:52.090
and their obligation to participate. And so I think in

14:52.130 --> 14:55.546
addition to the bylaw that we're going to establish

14:55.578 --> 14:59.466
or re establish today, I think it would be worthwhile for clerks

14:59.578 --> 15:03.082
to come back with potential educational awareness

15:03.226 --> 15:06.602
plans. I don't know, Chair Harris, if that could be a

15:06.626 --> 15:10.030
direction staff or if I should prepare a motion,

15:10.930 --> 15:14.554
but I think that would be very valuable in the year prior

15:14.682 --> 15:16.760
to an election cycle.

15:17.660 --> 15:21.092
Yeah. Thank you, Councillor Huinink. I believe staff are nodding their heads so they

15:21.116 --> 15:24.260
can take that back. So great. Thank you,

15:24.380 --> 15:25.560
Councillor Wolf.

15:29.340 --> 15:32.596
Through you, Mr. Chair. I know we're not supposed to debate

15:32.628 --> 15:36.132
with the delegate, though. I think it was opened up.

15:36.236 --> 15:40.164
I too am an educator and have taught for 38 years.

15:40.332 --> 15:44.342
And I was wondering if you were aware of the very active program

15:44.446 --> 15:48.006
Ontario student votes that we just had in this last election

15:48.158 --> 15:52.406
and how high schools and junior schools

15:52.518 --> 15:55.702
took part. The number of times as a

15:55.726 --> 15:59.730
city councillor in Cambridge, we had

16:00.270 --> 16:03.910
the civics classes from various high schools come and do

16:03.950 --> 16:08.118
mock councils. I do think that the education

16:08.254 --> 16:11.542
system is addressing that and what it means to be a citizen.

16:11.606 --> 16:15.626
If you go to a public school these days, instead of just a hallway,

16:15.718 --> 16:18.914
you see kindness hall, empathy hall,

16:19.042 --> 16:23.010
all of the values that make up

16:23.130 --> 16:26.930
a good citizen. I think that the education system

16:27.050 --> 16:29.986
is not to blame for our low voter turnout.

16:30.098 --> 16:33.938
Perhaps you should look at news. Where are

16:33.994 --> 16:37.554
our younger voters getting their news? They watch

16:37.642 --> 16:41.650
Netflix, they do podcasts. They're not Listening to

16:41.690 --> 16:45.500
CBC like I do every day, I think there are

16:45.540 --> 16:49.148
other reasons other than blaming

16:49.244 --> 16:52.604
our education system for low voter turnout

16:52.652 --> 16:56.380
and for not creating good citizens. Because what I see

16:56.420 --> 16:59.692
in the schools that I was contacted with and

16:59.716 --> 17:02.400
even looking at Colleen, who just ran,

17:03.300 --> 17:08.292
they often have debates for candidates.

17:08.476 --> 17:12.884
And I see more engagement from

17:12.972 --> 17:16.852
young people and students than I do from,

17:16.956 --> 17:21.347
let's say, young adults in their 20s,

17:21.488 --> 17:24.852
30s, than I would. And so I would say

17:24.876 --> 17:27.840
if we lowered the voter age while they're still in high school,

17:28.300 --> 17:32.280
we would increase voter turnout because those kids are engaged.

17:33.260 --> 17:36.284
Well, if I may, Councillor Wolf, I hear what

17:36.292 --> 17:39.708
you're saying and I'm heartened to hear what you said and I'm

17:39.724 --> 17:43.000
aware of what you were talking about. However,

17:43.380 --> 17:46.252
I talk to a lot of young people. I go out of my way to

17:46.276 --> 17:50.284
talk to young people and, and I ask them

17:50.372 --> 17:53.628
about what do you think about being a citizen?

17:53.804 --> 17:57.020
And I find that they do not know much about that.

17:57.060 --> 18:00.652
They have not thought about it. And I particularly did it

18:00.676 --> 18:04.314
this last go around when I was talking to young people in their 20s

18:04.362 --> 18:08.122
and 30s who were telling me they were not going to vote, they deliberately were

18:08.146 --> 18:11.290
not going to vote. And I simply say to them, well, what's your problem with

18:11.330 --> 18:14.058
being a citizen? And they said,

18:14.194 --> 18:17.978
oh. So I see a failure to connect

18:18.074 --> 18:22.314
the concept of citizen with the actual activity

18:22.362 --> 18:25.754
in politics, voting in politics, paying attention to politics

18:25.802 --> 18:29.540
in whatever way you're going to do it. And statistically we do

18:29.580 --> 18:33.236
know that across the liberal democracies,

18:33.348 --> 18:36.548
Waterloo region is not an exception to this, is that young

18:36.604 --> 18:40.244
people have much lower turnout rates than older

18:40.292 --> 18:43.892
people. And the fraction of people in, let's say their 20s

18:43.956 --> 18:47.684
who are voting in 2025 is generally

18:47.732 --> 18:51.476
lower than their counterparts 20 or 30 years ago. Another axiom

18:51.508 --> 18:55.236
in political science, once you start to vote, you continue to vote

18:55.268 --> 18:59.092
until your 70s. If you start late in life or the

18:59.116 --> 19:02.692
percentage is low and say your 20s, it's going to be lower in

19:02.716 --> 19:06.420
the 30s, 40s, 50s and 60s. That's well established

19:06.500 --> 19:09.640
life cycle voting pattern.

19:10.620 --> 19:14.228
Okay, I've got two more speakers. We will enter into

19:14.284 --> 19:17.668
a report and likely have a conversation about this here as well.

19:17.724 --> 19:21.524
So just direct your questions at the presenter directly

19:21.572 --> 19:24.982
about the, about his presentation and we'll keep it going. Sue,

19:25.126 --> 19:28.422
thank you. And this is not to debate the speakers. They've had their time to

19:28.446 --> 19:32.102
speak and I think we should go and talk about it in the council chambers

19:32.166 --> 19:35.654
now. So I was the

19:35.662 --> 19:39.302
one that suggested we relook at this because I thought it was

19:39.406 --> 19:43.478
more costly for A new person coming on and also

19:43.534 --> 19:46.982
for a new individual that was not recognized. Now people can say,

19:47.006 --> 19:49.174
you can go door to door and get people to put your signs on.

19:49.262 --> 19:52.374
From what I've heard in this past election, people had signs put on their lawn

19:52.422 --> 19:56.508
without their awareness. And there were multiple signs put on a lawn where

19:56.564 --> 19:59.820
they did not have permission. So that's a misconception right

19:59.860 --> 20:03.692
there. And people say, oh, signs are

20:03.716 --> 20:07.740
costly. My signs cost a fraction of what it cost

20:07.780 --> 20:10.720
me to put one ad in one newspaper.

20:11.380 --> 20:15.164
One ad in one newspaper or anything else cost ten times more,

20:15.252 --> 20:19.116
if not more than signs. But they are ugly.

20:19.308 --> 20:22.492
They don't get picked up. And my bylaw goes around and

20:22.516 --> 20:26.216
picks all the leftover signs. No debate about that. But it's

20:26.248 --> 20:29.432
about recognition. There were other candidates that ran just

20:29.456 --> 20:32.872
in this past election but had billboards all the way down highways

20:33.016 --> 20:36.488
because they could pay for those billboards. That's better than. I mean,

20:36.544 --> 20:39.752
if I could afford billboards down a highway, it's a

20:39.776 --> 20:43.160
lot more expensive than putting a sign up on a regional road,

20:43.280 --> 20:46.632
which probably won't get as much recognition as that giant billboard.

20:46.776 --> 20:50.056
So the thing being is, what I wanted for was something

20:50.128 --> 20:53.272
more fair and equitable. And what the people that I just

20:53.296 --> 20:56.790
heard speaking, it had nothing to do with fairness or about equitable.

20:56.870 --> 20:59.734
They don't like the signs and they think they're ugly. I don't know anybody that

20:59.742 --> 21:03.734
likes the signs. I don't even know any politician that likes putting the signs up.

21:03.902 --> 21:07.206
But I wanted to make sure that we allowed for every

21:07.278 --> 21:10.550
person that had the courage to

21:10.590 --> 21:14.038
put their name forward, had recognition.

21:14.134 --> 21:17.446
And you know what? They don't. They don't

21:17.478 --> 21:21.088
have the same. You've got to have money. You've got to have money for those

21:21.144 --> 21:24.464
television ads. You've got to have money for those billboards, you've got to

21:24.472 --> 21:28.176
have money for. Putting the newspaper ads

21:28.208 --> 21:31.232
in the system, I guess, is always going to be

21:31.256 --> 21:35.264
unfair. And I don't know if putting regional signs up on regional roads

21:35.312 --> 21:38.384
makes it any more fair. I thought it'd give more recognition, but that's all.

21:38.472 --> 21:41.872
So I'm one of the ones that brought this back because I wanted to make

21:41.896 --> 21:45.888
it more fair. Because all of us sitting here have an unfair

21:45.904 --> 21:49.494
advantage. Maybe not because people do recognize

21:49.542 --> 21:51.542
us, and if you don't like us, you know you're not going to vote for

21:51.566 --> 21:54.998
us. But we are recognizable. The new

21:55.054 --> 21:58.598
person coming in isn't. So that's what I wanted the discussion for.

21:58.654 --> 22:02.662
I wanted to be open discussion. I didn't Want one side.

22:02.846 --> 22:06.534
I wanted to look at the whole thing and think about it logically.

22:06.662 --> 22:10.406
And odds are we probably won't keep this regional signs on the regional

22:10.438 --> 22:14.782
roads. But I wanted the discussion because that's what democracy is,

22:14.886 --> 22:18.798
an open discussion. Thank you. Okay.

22:18.974 --> 22:23.198
Councillor James, thank you. Are we talking to the report now?

22:23.334 --> 22:26.638
No, we are still asking questions of the delegate.

22:26.734 --> 22:30.654
Okay. So in. Because we have one more delegation. Okay. And just to the delegates

22:30.702 --> 22:33.966
who spoke. I think there is a shift that is happening where

22:34.038 --> 22:37.486
signs are not necessarily going to be as impactful

22:37.518 --> 22:41.044
as things like digital moving forward. Forward. Having just gone through this.

22:41.132 --> 22:44.148
And I think when we look at signs,

22:44.244 --> 22:48.052
we're thinking of a historical way of campaigning.

22:48.196 --> 22:51.844
And I can say that this is. There is a shift

22:51.892 --> 22:55.140
that's going to happen that's going to change, especially as we move to more digital.

22:55.260 --> 22:58.564
So I'll speak to the report as it comes up. But I also want to

22:58.572 --> 23:01.476
say that our staff does an excellent job of going into schools.

23:01.508 --> 23:04.362
When asked at grade for the starting at grade five.

23:04.516 --> 23:08.718
I know I've spoken to students as of early as grade one

23:08.854 --> 23:12.638
about civics and engagement and about their voting and

23:12.694 --> 23:16.382
democracy and also as an educator incorporating that into the

23:16.406 --> 23:20.078
classrooms that I was teaching. And so I think the apathy around

23:20.134 --> 23:23.534
voting has to come from some of the tomfoolery we see with some elected

23:23.582 --> 23:27.406
officials across the border or wherever and their

23:27.478 --> 23:30.718
lack of distrust. So I'll speak to the report when it comes along,

23:30.774 --> 23:33.580
but. But they'll stop for now.

23:38.360 --> 23:42.016
I don't see any more questions. Peter, I appreciate your time. I know

23:42.168 --> 23:46.000
everyone knows here you're a professor and I was nodded here.

23:46.040 --> 23:49.392
Our regional clerk is a student or was a student of

23:49.416 --> 23:53.232
yours. So I think you've done fairly well with what

23:53.256 --> 23:56.704
we've got here ourselves. So thank you for presenting and I'll call upon

23:56.752 --> 23:59.940
our next delegation. Brendan.

24:01.120 --> 24:04.856
I believe Brennan. And for those that just came in, lots of seats,

24:04.968 --> 24:08.456
so no need to stand. Have us have yourself a seat and.

24:08.608 --> 24:10.540
Yeah, Brendan, you've got five minutes.

24:12.320 --> 24:15.656
Thank you. My name is Brendan Sherratt. I'm a resident

24:15.688 --> 24:19.288
of Kitchener and I come here with the perspective of

24:19.344 --> 24:22.600
an active participant in election campaigns at all levels,

24:22.680 --> 24:26.166
from a voter to a volunteer to a campaign manager.

24:26.288 --> 24:30.074
And I'm here to speak against the change to the rules around election

24:30.162 --> 24:34.362
signs. Signs on public land are wasteful and don't represent

24:34.546 --> 24:38.090
the candidate. Local candidate support. We found

24:38.130 --> 24:43.002
that in the report. It indicates that the amendment

24:43.066 --> 24:46.550
is. The amendment is working from

24:46.850 --> 24:50.714
2018 with 683 electoral signs removed

24:50.842 --> 24:54.544
with an 83% decrease resulting

24:54.592 --> 24:57.936
in 115 being removed in the last election.

24:58.088 --> 25:01.580
That is success. And when the region is trying to save money,

25:01.960 --> 25:05.472
I believe everything that we can do to reduce the

25:05.576 --> 25:09.280
load is beneficial to support some

25:09.320 --> 25:12.720
people. Signs don't vote.

25:12.800 --> 25:16.640
People vote. Election signs on public property

25:16.720 --> 25:20.868
do not reflect voter sentiment. It shows

25:20.884 --> 25:24.900
that a candidate can afford more signs. And yes, signs are not as expensive

25:24.980 --> 25:29.108
as record ads. But signs in mass quantities add

25:29.164 --> 25:32.100
up and they can add up significantly.

25:32.260 --> 25:35.812
The candidate that is focusing on their signs, they're not

25:35.836 --> 25:40.120
focusing on knocking on doors, they're not focusing on engaging with voters in different ways.

25:40.700 --> 25:44.798
It also encourages the sign wars and I think we've all seen the

25:44.854 --> 25:48.782
poor effects of sign wars. Candidates placing signs that

25:48.806 --> 25:51.774
block each other, supporting supporters,

25:51.822 --> 25:55.374
moving opponents, signs taking them down, complaining to bylaw

25:55.422 --> 25:57.810
enforcement, vandalism.

25:58.230 --> 26:03.806
That's creating hostility. It's creating an environment where campaigns

26:03.838 --> 26:07.822
are fighting against each other instead of focusing on votes and

26:07.846 --> 26:11.646
then the environmental impact. The council just spoke

26:11.678 --> 26:15.474
about some of their goals and one one of their goals being reducing

26:15.602 --> 26:19.186
waste with signs being difficult to

26:19.338 --> 26:22.882
recycle, if not impossible. I believe that

26:22.986 --> 26:26.390
encouraging additional signs, especially large ones,

26:26.970 --> 26:30.962
just increases. That doesn't help

26:30.986 --> 26:35.410
us towards achieving that goal. It's a poor use of public resources.

26:35.570 --> 26:39.410
Staff have clearly stated the significant time that it takes to

26:39.450 --> 26:42.482
deal with those signs and I think that we would all agree that we would

26:42.506 --> 26:46.216
rather staff working on focusing on other core things

26:46.288 --> 26:48.860
other than measuring the distance between signs.

26:49.280 --> 26:52.744
Road safety we all care about road safety and

26:52.832 --> 26:56.540
election signs create that visual clutter that distracts drivers,

26:58.880 --> 27:02.456
especially in high traffic areas and intersections where all signs

27:02.488 --> 27:06.008
want to be. Campaign volunteers are putting

27:06.024 --> 27:09.192
themselves at risk, often pulling over on the side of the road to

27:09.216 --> 27:12.810
put up a sign and and creating an additional distraction both for

27:12.850 --> 27:16.890
other motorists and a danger to themselves. And then traffic safety.

27:16.970 --> 27:20.874
Studies have shown that there's an additional cognitive overload with

27:20.962 --> 27:24.666
additional signs that are along roads. So the more signs

27:24.698 --> 27:28.922
we have, the more reduced time to stop,

27:29.106 --> 27:32.474
which we obviously want everyone to focus on.

27:32.642 --> 27:35.470
Modern campaigning is not election signs.

27:36.050 --> 27:39.488
Election signs as indicated are

27:39.674 --> 27:43.492
one form of engaging, but participating in social media,

27:43.596 --> 27:46.964
being there in town halls, direct engagement

27:47.012 --> 27:50.740
on social platforms like Reddit and Facebook and all these

27:50.780 --> 27:54.320
other things are ways to engage with voters. And we

27:55.420 --> 27:59.380
signs are not the only way. So in conclusion, I believe that

27:59.420 --> 28:03.716
the 2021 election sign prohibit prohibition

28:03.748 --> 28:06.460
is working. It's reducing waste and environmental harm,

28:06.580 --> 28:10.844
eliminating sign related conflicts. It's freeing up municipal enforcement

28:10.892 --> 28:14.892
resources, improving road safety and creating a fairer election process.

28:15.076 --> 28:19.340
There's no compelling evidence that election signs on public lands improve voter

28:19.420 --> 28:23.164
engagement and the committee should uphold the current bylaw.

28:23.212 --> 28:26.880
Thank you very much. Okay, we have a question. Councillor Foxton,

28:28.980 --> 28:32.172
just a comment, Chair, and I was going to mention it with

28:32.196 --> 28:35.844
the report. So it's listed the number of signs that bylaw

28:35.932 --> 28:39.588
pulled from the last election or the previous one.

28:39.724 --> 28:43.828
And I'd like to note that my township pulled

28:44.004 --> 28:47.172
hundreds of signs. The region had asked them to pick them up for

28:47.196 --> 28:50.596
them. And then when we asked where we should take them, they said, you guys

28:50.628 --> 28:54.164
get rid of them. So next time that we do that, we're going to

28:54.172 --> 28:57.412
be billing the region because by law for the region does not come out to

28:57.436 --> 29:00.880
the townships. They don't do it and it costs my people.

29:01.260 --> 29:05.060
So just a heads up for that. Also, I resent that

29:05.100 --> 29:09.000
people think that politicians put up a sign and don't go door to door.

29:09.340 --> 29:12.692
I would like to almost swear and say that's, I'll use the

29:12.716 --> 29:16.180
term crap instead because I am a one

29:16.220 --> 29:19.844
legged person that goes to every single door as

29:19.852 --> 29:22.916
well as putting up signs. That's the end of my comments. Thank you, Chair.

29:22.988 --> 29:26.884
Okay, questions of the delegation. Councillor McCabe,

29:26.932 --> 29:28.800
thank you. Thanks for coming in,

29:29.430 --> 29:32.926
Brendan, and sharing your thoughts on this and to the other delegates

29:32.958 --> 29:36.302
as well. I'm just curious and I hear what you're saying about the

29:36.326 --> 29:40.046
signs and that and I do agree with it. I'm just curious, since you're here,

29:40.118 --> 29:44.414
if you could maybe provide some of your insights

29:44.462 --> 29:46.170
or thoughts on like,

29:48.070 --> 29:51.770
what would it be of assistance to people who are trying to,

29:52.310 --> 29:55.758
who are trying to get elected, who are not incumbents, maybe who are from

29:55.814 --> 29:59.674
equity deserving groups. Like what sort of advice do you think

29:59.762 --> 30:02.250
if, if signs aren't going to be one of the options?

30:02.330 --> 30:05.594
What, you know, how do they, how do

30:05.602 --> 30:09.162
they break into this, this game? Yeah. Point of

30:09.186 --> 30:13.066
order, Chair. Go ahead, Councillor Foxton.

30:13.258 --> 30:17.258
Should we not stick to the procedure of talking about the signed bylaw?

30:17.354 --> 30:20.458
If someone wants to get election information,

30:20.594 --> 30:23.578
maybe they can go to somebody else at another time.

30:23.714 --> 30:27.578
Well, you know, it is, it is the ability for

30:27.634 --> 30:30.970
a councillor to ask a delegate a question of his presentation.

30:31.050 --> 30:33.946
That's, that's what we're on now. I mean, we're going to get to the report

30:34.018 --> 30:37.546
and I think we're going to have a broader conversation about the staff report.

30:37.618 --> 30:41.470
But you know, I'm going to allow a question of a delegation,

30:42.610 --> 30:45.830
so you can answer. Thank you.

30:47.730 --> 30:50.762
I believe that the, the stat is that 90% of

30:50.786 --> 30:54.666
incumbents are reelected. So There is not a fair playing field.

30:54.818 --> 30:58.790
And it is the fact the way to

30:59.170 --> 31:02.762
engage with people is to start from the ground, to talk

31:02.786 --> 31:06.634
with people, to engage with people. And I apologize, I did not mean to imply

31:06.762 --> 31:09.626
that you were not going door to door,

31:09.658 --> 31:12.550
but door to door canvassing, which takes a lot of time,

31:12.930 --> 31:16.602
is one of those ways. And it's the small conversations,

31:16.666 --> 31:20.590
those table conversations, those engaging online with

31:20.630 --> 31:24.450
people and making yourself seen and heard and valued.

31:25.350 --> 31:28.494
So that is the thing that you can do to

31:28.502 --> 31:30.850
get engaged with voters.

31:31.430 --> 31:34.410
Election signs are one of those things, but they're not the only thing.

31:35.590 --> 31:38.958
Chair Redman Brendan, I really want to thank

31:38.974 --> 31:43.230
you for coming in and I know that you've got lots of organizational

31:43.310 --> 31:47.056
experience and on the ground in campaigns. You mentioned Reddit

31:47.238 --> 31:50.652
and I guess again this is connected to the signage and the

31:50.676 --> 31:54.332
fact that you feel that the current bylaw should stay in force.

31:54.476 --> 31:57.836
And I just wonder if you could comment. From my political

31:57.988 --> 32:02.252
experience, social media has absolutely usurped

32:02.316 --> 32:05.160
so many other ways, whether it's pamphlets or things.

32:06.260 --> 32:09.640
As a relatively young person who is

32:09.940 --> 32:13.646
very involved in campaigns, what would your. I just,

32:13.718 --> 32:17.166
you mentioned Reddit and I'm thinking, can you just talk about how we're

32:17.198 --> 32:21.326
progressing to be more of social media and moving forward in future

32:21.398 --> 32:24.670
campaigns? Yeah, I think it was quite amazing to see in the last

32:24.710 --> 32:28.014
provincial election the amount of candidates that engaged and used.

32:28.102 --> 32:31.822
Reddit is just one platform. It's an online platform where you can engage,

32:31.886 --> 32:35.774
communicate and vote up and down, but it was a great way of basically

32:35.822 --> 32:38.942
having a virtual town hall. It's not always fair, it's not

32:38.966 --> 32:42.490
always great, but it allows that

32:43.510 --> 32:48.050
free, you're only giving your time way of engaging with voters.

32:49.190 --> 32:51.876
And unlike signs, which may cost $15,

32:52.032 --> 32:55.742
$5 depending on where you are per sign,

32:55.926 --> 32:59.022
this is a way of engaging with those voters and hearing them

32:59.046 --> 33:02.190
out. And similar to

33:02.230 --> 33:05.172
a round table conversation,

33:05.366 --> 33:09.048
you're listening to those, those voters and hearing their,

33:09.104 --> 33:12.536
their, their feedback and they're hearing from you as well in an unfiltered

33:12.568 --> 33:14.900
way. Thank you so much for coming in.

33:15.440 --> 33:18.660
Okay, thank you very much for coming in.

33:20.080 --> 33:23.752
Oh, Councillor James just popped up. Councillor James,

33:23.896 --> 33:27.688
thank you. Just through you, Chair, to speak to some of the social

33:27.744 --> 33:31.016
media platforms and recognizing that oftentimes

33:31.048 --> 33:34.760
it may not be the candidate that people are actually speaking to depending on their

33:34.800 --> 33:38.428
team. So I think we need to really more nuanced

33:38.524 --> 33:41.724
when it comes to things like social media platforms because yes,

33:41.772 --> 33:44.876
it may seem as though there's direct contact with someone,

33:44.948 --> 33:48.684
but the realities are the amount of resources Somebody

33:48.732 --> 33:52.540
has will enable a team person or someone to actually be

33:52.580 --> 33:55.628
responding and it may appear like there's direct connection.

33:55.724 --> 33:59.244
So I mean with all of this there's different ways to campaign and different ways

33:59.292 --> 34:03.212
to engage. Signs are one way. But I think as we

34:03.236 --> 34:06.732
get into a digital way and social media way, also know that all of

34:06.756 --> 34:10.374
the harm and things that can happen on social, social media and the appearance

34:10.422 --> 34:13.942
and presumption like it is direct connection when the reality is

34:13.966 --> 34:17.462
it may not be so just adding a perspective in there as well.

34:17.646 --> 34:21.446
Councillor. No, I want to speak

34:21.478 --> 34:24.918
to the board. Okay, we'll get you on, I think we'll

34:24.934 --> 34:28.102
get you on that report when we get to it. So thank you,

34:28.126 --> 34:32.550
Brennan. Appreciate you coming in. Colleagues, we'll move on to the consent agenda.

34:32.710 --> 34:36.214
If you wish to remove an item from the consent agenda, please indicate.

34:36.262 --> 34:40.322
So now do

34:40.346 --> 34:43.106
we have a mover and a second to approve the consent agenda moved by Councillor

34:43.138 --> 34:46.786
Foxton and seconded by Councillor McCabe. Please use your yes

34:46.818 --> 34:48.270
or no button, please.

34:53.290 --> 34:57.430
I'm a yes. I'm a yes. I'm just having a technical difficulty,

35:10.500 --> 35:11.840
Councillor Liggett.

35:17.380 --> 35:21.964
Okay, colleagues, that's carried. We'll move on to the agenda.

35:22.012 --> 35:25.756
Item six, one strategic priority, resilient and future ready organization.

35:25.948 --> 35:29.392
Oh, did I miss? No, that was consent. So you're off

35:29.416 --> 35:31.060
the hook. You're off the hook, Wayne.

35:33.400 --> 35:36.816
Number seven, the regular agenda, Strategic priority, resilient and future ready

35:36.848 --> 35:40.460
organization. Election signed by law.

35:42.520 --> 35:45.808
We're back at it. We'll call on William Short, will the

35:45.864 --> 35:49.360
regional clerk to introduce the report. William?

35:49.520 --> 35:53.072
Thank you Chair Harris. Council will recall a

35:53.096 --> 35:57.082
number of months back Councillor Foxton asked staff to to look into

35:57.266 --> 36:01.050
and to bring back a report on the election signed

36:01.130 --> 36:05.274
bylaw. Specifically, there are a couple of questions surrounding voter

36:05.322 --> 36:09.450
turnout in relation to any statistics or data that was able

36:09.490 --> 36:13.770
to be found by staff on whether or not signs

36:13.930 --> 36:17.070
increase or decrease voter turnout. Unfortunately,

36:17.970 --> 36:21.322
specifically to that information there wasn't any data available at

36:21.346 --> 36:25.040
Elections Ontario or through AMO to collect.

36:25.200 --> 36:28.448
We pulled together the last two reports

36:28.504 --> 36:32.928
that were on this in 2012. I believe there was an

36:32.984 --> 36:36.672
original sign or 2010, 2012 there was an original sign bylaw. It was

36:36.696 --> 36:39.792
then amended in 2018 and then amended again in the last term

36:39.816 --> 36:44.064
of Council in 2021 to completely ban election signs

36:44.112 --> 36:48.016
on regional roads. And we talked

36:48.048 --> 36:51.288
to our bylaw staff and I think there were some questions around

36:51.344 --> 36:54.872
whether or not there would be any more work

36:54.976 --> 36:58.088
one way or another for our staff. Whether the signed bylaw

36:58.104 --> 37:01.752
state as it was or if it was in its original form and from our

37:01.776 --> 37:05.288
perspective, staff had the same number of calls

37:05.384 --> 37:08.872
whether it was around removing them from regional property,

37:08.936 --> 37:12.632
from regional roads or measuring the distance between one

37:12.656 --> 37:15.824
election sign to another. So from staff's perspective,

37:15.912 --> 37:19.600
perspective, this is really a regional council decision whether or not they

37:19.640 --> 37:23.488
want to keep the ban of sign by, of signs on,

37:23.624 --> 37:26.832
on regional roads as is or revert back to the,

37:26.936 --> 37:31.184
to the original bylaw as it was before it was amended in 2021.

37:31.352 --> 37:34.592
So the, the information from both of

37:34.616 --> 37:37.264
those previous reports was put into,

37:37.432 --> 37:40.496
into this for council and we're

37:40.528 --> 37:43.800
at council's hands with what they would like to do.

37:46.580 --> 37:49.772
Okay, Councillor James, thank you. Through you,

37:49.796 --> 37:52.924
Chair Harris, thanks for this report. Before I

37:53.012 --> 37:56.396
get into this report, I do need to point some things

37:56.468 --> 38:00.172
out as I read what was brought forward in 2021 and had

38:00.196 --> 38:03.804
I been here as a staff person at the time, I would have flagged

38:03.852 --> 38:07.228
this way before. But I want to make it clear for those who are

38:07.284 --> 38:11.832
running and reading this report report, there are some very bad stereotypes

38:11.896 --> 38:15.144
that have been in my opinion been made public

38:15.232 --> 38:18.520
about barriers and while running.

38:18.600 --> 38:21.752
And it has to do with certain things around and

38:21.776 --> 38:25.832
I'm going to verbatim say this. Racialized and marginalized groups disproportionately

38:25.896 --> 38:28.940
live with low income that create a barrier.

38:29.360 --> 38:32.520
Very bad stereotype. And I know, I just want to flag this

38:32.560 --> 38:35.876
because I know it's not related to the actual report, but it has very

38:35.948 --> 38:40.068
deeply rooted systemic barriers and stereotypings that can

38:40.124 --> 38:43.840
impact future racialized or

38:44.380 --> 38:47.956
marginalized groups who are running. And I want to just debunk

38:47.988 --> 38:51.844
this myth. There's also language in here that speaks

38:51.892 --> 38:55.172
to low income and voters.

38:55.236 --> 38:59.600
Political ideology tends to be socialist, which just

38:59.990 --> 39:03.422
so, so, so harmful when

39:03.446 --> 39:07.694
it comes to signs, you know, to think about racialized

39:07.742 --> 39:12.110
or marginalized groups from just strictly a low financial

39:12.230 --> 39:15.758
barrier, I don't think that is the thing to focus on when

39:15.814 --> 39:18.990
it comes to just finances. I think we do have to think about things like

39:19.030 --> 39:22.222
racism and discrimination. We also, I mean the

39:22.246 --> 39:25.614
biggest thing for me was putting my picture on the sign or not putting the

39:25.622 --> 39:29.190
picture on the, on the sign. So when

39:29.230 --> 39:33.014
it comes to the disparities that exist, I don't want to focus

39:33.102 --> 39:36.550
in on just the equity perspective from

39:36.590 --> 39:40.806
a racialized, marginalized lens and recognizing

39:40.838 --> 39:44.582
that if we're speaking to what was brought forward before, recognize some

39:44.606 --> 39:48.694
of the harm that was brought in this because it was painful

39:48.742 --> 39:52.422
to see that this was put out in public because it feeds into the systemic

39:52.486 --> 39:57.654
racism and discrimination that if you are racialized

39:57.702 --> 40:01.638
or otherwise low socioeconomic, that the financial is the only

40:01.694 --> 40:04.726
barrier to running an office.

40:04.798 --> 40:08.198
So I just wanna caution this. As for

40:08.254 --> 40:11.570
the signs, like I said, the signs to me do not.

40:12.990 --> 40:16.290
They have an impact, but they are not all.

40:17.950 --> 40:21.718
They are not a guarantee. And I do know colleagues who had

40:21.774 --> 40:25.126
50 signs put out versus 900 or 2,000

40:25.198 --> 40:28.676
signs instead still got elected. I think if we're

40:28.708 --> 40:31.988
looking at it just from one singular way, it's a very

40:32.044 --> 40:35.920
historic way of running a campaign. And I think there has to be

40:36.220 --> 40:40.600
looking at things from that digital perspective

40:41.020 --> 40:44.804
as we move forward. But I'll stop there. But I just want to

40:44.812 --> 40:47.876
recognize for colleagues around the table that reading this report,

40:47.948 --> 40:51.236
it's very much indicative of some of the challenges we're

40:51.268 --> 40:54.580
trying to navigate as it comes to being seen

40:54.620 --> 40:57.988
as equal and not as oppressed based

40:58.044 --> 41:01.508
on financials. Oppression can look and can take many different

41:01.564 --> 41:05.092
types of forms. So I'll kind of stop there and

41:05.116 --> 41:08.440
let others go through the queue. Councillor wolf,

41:09.980 --> 41:13.044
through you, Mr. Chair. I agree.

41:13.132 --> 41:16.804
I'm glad that Councillor Foxton brought this motion

41:16.852 --> 41:22.110
forward or this requested this, because I do

41:22.150 --> 41:25.726
believe that the status quo really supports

41:25.758 --> 41:29.502
the incumbent. And I think that in

41:29.526 --> 41:33.006
all of the campaigns that I've been involved in, and I've

41:33.038 --> 41:36.330
actually run in seven, I think,

41:36.710 --> 41:38.970
and successful in five,

41:40.790 --> 41:45.410
and helped on many campaigns, having signs,

41:45.900 --> 41:49.940
public property is important. Name recognition

41:50.100 --> 41:54.516
is extremely important. And as Councillor Foxton

41:54.548 --> 41:58.820
pointed out, billboards, electronic signs are

41:58.860 --> 42:02.356
extremely expensive and they're also privately

42:02.388 --> 42:05.840
owned. And I think that

42:07.180 --> 42:11.406
we need to have some public space where people

42:11.478 --> 42:15.230
running in an election can express themselves. I do

42:15.270 --> 42:18.798
agree with limiting the number of signs on a

42:18.934 --> 42:22.926
public road saying, you know, you're allowed. You know, I guess what

42:23.078 --> 42:26.846
it was was between this

42:26.918 --> 42:30.530
part and this part, you're allowed two signs or whatever.

42:31.030 --> 42:35.470
I think that was important to cut

42:35.510 --> 42:44.670
down a little bit on the visual problems.

42:44.750 --> 42:48.110
But I think that having the, having the ability

42:48.270 --> 42:52.110
to put your name out there on a sign is

42:52.150 --> 42:56.126
one of the cheapest ways of getting some

42:56.278 --> 42:59.502
recognition. And again, social media,

42:59.606 --> 43:02.942
the digital, that's a very dangerous

43:03.006 --> 43:07.438
world, as all of us know and

43:07.494 --> 43:10.010
for many, many reasons. And again,

43:10.470 --> 43:14.078
we'll never have complete fairness because

43:14.134 --> 43:17.390
of the different amount of money that

43:17.430 --> 43:20.750
people get to spend. And that's why I think

43:20.790 --> 43:24.414
it is important that in the public realm we try to

43:24.502 --> 43:27.982
make an even playing field. So limiting the

43:28.006 --> 43:31.382
number of signs. But everybody has that's running can put

43:31.406 --> 43:35.606
a sign up and allowing some of that name recognition.

43:35.798 --> 43:39.350
And you know, as an incumbent, I know I have an Advantage,

43:39.510 --> 43:43.574
even frankly in a sign with Pam Wolf short

43:43.662 --> 43:47.670
names. My name

43:47.710 --> 43:51.490
can be big. But you know, I think that.

43:52.270 --> 43:55.846
Again, I appreciate that sue

43:55.878 --> 43:59.740
brought this up and I would like us to consider

44:00.520 --> 44:03.620
maybe going back to our original bylaw.

44:04.360 --> 44:07.920
Councillor. Thank you, Mr. Chair.

44:08.040 --> 44:12.224
And I note this report is for information purposes only.

44:12.312 --> 44:16.416
So I'm not sure what action you're expecting.

44:16.608 --> 44:20.544
My preference would be. And if it needs to be a motion that

44:20.712 --> 44:24.230
no action be taken, I support

44:25.170 --> 44:29.434
our current bylaw. I think election signs

44:29.482 --> 44:33.466
are becoming an antiquated way of trying to communicate

44:33.498 --> 44:37.910
with people with so many online options that are available today.

44:39.650 --> 44:44.190
Most of the election signs today are made of a non biodegradable

44:46.050 --> 44:49.762
product called Choroplast. It gets into

44:49.866 --> 44:53.682
our landfill site and doesn't decompose. It's contrary

44:53.746 --> 44:57.298
to one of the

44:57.434 --> 45:00.910
strategic priorities of the region of

45:01.210 --> 45:03.970
reduce, reuse and recycle,

45:04.130 --> 45:07.970
which includes plastic. So I think by permitting them on our property,

45:08.130 --> 45:11.910
we're in contravention of one of our strategic plans.

45:14.100 --> 45:17.404
Nobody can like the general appearance and clutter.

45:17.532 --> 45:21.068
Signs get put up, they get broken, they're on one leg.

45:21.204 --> 45:24.524
I think it just distracts the attention

45:24.572 --> 45:28.060
of drivers and it's just not a pleasant sight.

45:28.220 --> 45:31.612
And I think there's some liability involved with volunteers

45:31.676 --> 45:34.956
on busy regional roads putting these signs up,

45:35.108 --> 45:38.044
parking their car on the side of the road while they're rushing,

45:38.092 --> 45:41.388
putting up signs. And there's some safety issues.

45:41.444 --> 45:44.794
I think for the people that are doing that, there's distinctly

45:44.842 --> 45:47.802
an advantage in my mind to incumbents.

45:47.946 --> 45:51.466
They have the money, they have the signs left over from the previous elections.

45:51.498 --> 45:55.050
They know the best locations, they got the people to put them up.

45:55.170 --> 45:58.826
And so I think it's a distinct advantage

45:58.858 --> 46:02.202
to those for incumbents. But I think most

46:02.226 --> 46:05.710
important for me is they don't belong on public property.

46:08.130 --> 46:11.656
If you want to put up an election sign, put it up in somebody's private

46:11.728 --> 46:15.140
property, on a front lawn or at a business where it shows support.

46:15.440 --> 46:19.112
But public property is not designed

46:19.256 --> 46:23.112
to advertise for political candidates. There are private

46:23.296 --> 46:27.304
right of ways they interfere with regional and municipal staffs

46:27.352 --> 46:30.296
mowing and getting around the signs in the fall.

46:30.368 --> 46:34.072
But they just have no place on public property. The support needs to be

46:34.096 --> 46:37.618
shown on private property. So my.

46:37.754 --> 46:41.122
I, I would. If you want a motion, I would move that no action be

46:41.146 --> 46:41.710
taken.

46:44.170 --> 46:46.710
Okay, thank you, Councillor. Councillor Liggett.

46:48.570 --> 46:52.370
Wow. It's a great conversation. I would support going back

46:52.410 --> 46:55.666
as well. So for those who are

46:55.738 --> 46:59.506
starting out to

46:59.578 --> 47:02.590
try to become a representative of the people,

47:02.950 --> 47:06.766
they don't Have a lot of private lawns to be putting

47:06.878 --> 47:10.046
signs on. So when we take away public

47:10.118 --> 47:13.134
lands, that's their one opportunity to have their,

47:13.222 --> 47:16.734
their names out there. I think when all of us started out,

47:16.902 --> 47:19.770
we didn't have a lot of money to,

47:20.870 --> 47:24.462
to buy the, like hundreds and

47:24.486 --> 47:27.940
hundreds of signs. We didn't have the support of

47:28.110 --> 47:32.216
donations because people didn't know who we were. And that's

47:32.248 --> 47:36.568
on the, on the candidate to make sure they get out there and

47:36.624 --> 47:39.656
think about it long in advance of,

47:39.728 --> 47:42.872
of when they put their names out, of getting involved in

47:42.896 --> 47:46.616
the community, sitting on committees, writing letters to the editor so that people

47:46.688 --> 47:50.008
know what their philosophy is, what their thoughts are on

47:50.064 --> 47:53.454
different issues. And so that's on them as part of the homework.

47:53.632 --> 47:57.778
Nothing replaces knocking on doors. And we all know that

47:57.914 --> 48:01.698
that's the thing that we need to do. And thank you

48:01.754 --> 48:05.154
so much, Colleen, for bringing forward the

48:05.242 --> 48:08.642
discrimination in this. I would never have supported this report back

48:08.666 --> 48:12.306
there. The minute I read this, I looked at this and I thought, oh,

48:12.338 --> 48:15.990
my God, who put this together? It's so discriminatory.

48:18.010 --> 48:20.550
It just sets everybody up for failure.

48:21.320 --> 48:24.976
And even people who, you know, it talks about names

48:25.008 --> 48:28.576
that are not Anglicized. Well, I would say that with the number of people

48:28.648 --> 48:31.728
that have come into this country since this report has been

48:31.784 --> 48:35.472
done, that that that's changed a little

48:35.496 --> 48:39.280
bit. But yet we're still seeing this in this report that we're looking

48:39.320 --> 48:43.360
at today. All of these discriminatory comments are

48:43.400 --> 48:46.768
still sitting in the background. I don't think this should have been part of this

48:46.824 --> 48:50.556
report because it just, it brings

48:50.588 --> 48:54.476
this forward again. So I'm just going to switch

48:54.668 --> 48:58.348
a little bit of feet here, talking about

48:58.484 --> 49:02.800
some of the comments that were brought up when the speaker were presenting.

49:03.780 --> 49:07.276
I go to the schools on a regular basis,

49:07.468 --> 49:10.908
but I find that it's always the same schools and that's

49:10.924 --> 49:15.574
on the board of education when they have teachers

49:15.622 --> 49:19.222
in their classrooms who, who teach civics. So you go and you speak to the

49:19.246 --> 49:22.422
grade five classes and you go and speak to the high school classes.

49:22.566 --> 49:25.974
It's always the same ones. And yet look at how many schools

49:26.022 --> 49:29.814
we have in this region. There's very, very few who,

49:29.982 --> 49:33.750
who actually teach it. And I

49:33.790 --> 49:37.814
am always appalled by the lack of knowledge of the different levels

49:37.942 --> 49:41.690
of, of the public. So if we're looking for good candidates

49:42.280 --> 49:45.392
later on, the ones who will take possession of

49:45.416 --> 49:49.088
their responsibility, write the letters to the editor, come before

49:49.144 --> 49:52.832
council and speak, get their names out to be good candidates in

49:52.856 --> 49:56.512
the future. It starts in the school system And I

49:56.536 --> 50:00.096
know Mr. Piatkowski was a delegate.

50:00.208 --> 50:03.920
I asked him to get behind this and have the

50:04.040 --> 50:07.760
teachers have us or somebody who

50:07.800 --> 50:11.320
knows about the system come to the classes. It should be

50:11.360 --> 50:15.288
part of the curriculum. And until we get

50:15.344 --> 50:19.528
that going, I think we're going to be looking at another

50:19.584 --> 50:22.600
whole generation before they're educated enough to be.

50:22.720 --> 50:26.232
To be voting at the age of 16. And I think that's going

50:26.256 --> 50:29.848
to take, you know, maybe if we started it next September, the kids

50:29.864 --> 50:32.600
that are going to kindergarten now, by the time they get to that age,

50:32.640 --> 50:36.146
if they were year after year after year taught this stuff and it's

50:36.178 --> 50:39.522
spoken about in. In their homes, then we can get to

50:39.546 --> 50:41.710
that. But right now, heck, no.

50:42.890 --> 50:45.970
Sorry, I just went on and off on a little bit of a tangent there,

50:46.010 --> 50:49.270
but there you go. Councillor Vrbanovic,

50:51.770 --> 50:55.282
thank you very much, Chair Harris. And I

50:55.306 --> 50:59.106
guess there's no motion on the floor yet, but there. Councillor Erb

50:59.138 --> 51:02.270
indicated he was moving no action.

51:03.690 --> 51:06.882
So I presume that will be the first motion that will

51:06.906 --> 51:10.110
be considered. The.

51:11.690 --> 51:15.410
If that motion is moved and seconded,

51:15.490 --> 51:17.830
and if that motion isn't successful,

51:18.570 --> 51:23.138
I certainly would be prepared to move a motion to have

51:23.194 --> 51:26.962
us allow signs on

51:27.066 --> 51:30.800
regional property. Again, I think it could be a limited

51:30.880 --> 51:34.272
number if that is part of

51:34.456 --> 51:36.620
the situation. However,

51:37.720 --> 51:41.520
just adding some of my colleagues really said a lot of the things

51:41.560 --> 51:44.832
that I think needed to be said for new candidates.

51:44.896 --> 51:48.288
In particular, having the ability to

51:48.344 --> 51:51.856
put signs out there when you don't

51:51.888 --> 51:55.184
have a big budget to work with is one of

51:55.192 --> 51:59.272
the initial ways that you can get your name out there and be considered for

51:59.376 --> 52:02.552
electoral office. The reality is that our

52:02.576 --> 52:04.180
region is a large region.

52:05.520 --> 52:09.864
The, you know, the people need

52:09.952 --> 52:13.220
significant resources in order to. To get known.

52:13.600 --> 52:17.784
And I think if we're truly committed to seeing

52:17.832 --> 52:21.448
a broader range of people, you know,

52:21.504 --> 52:25.480
seeking public office, then we

52:25.520 --> 52:29.180
need to facilitate mechanisms in order to

52:29.640 --> 52:33.152
help make that happen. And so, you know,

52:33.176 --> 52:36.800
I wasn't supportive of us going this direction in the first place with

52:36.840 --> 52:39.792
signs. And I think we need to.

52:39.976 --> 52:44.896
I think we need to go back to actually

52:45.048 --> 52:48.416
allowing them going forward. So if Councillor

52:48.448 --> 52:51.216
Erb's motion is not successful,

52:51.408 --> 52:55.256
I'm prepared to move one to have us

52:55.408 --> 52:58.952
reinstate them. Thank you. Yeah. So, Councillor Vrbanovic, there is

52:59.056 --> 53:03.100
no action would require no motion whatsoever.

53:03.520 --> 53:07.500
And so if you're prepared to bring forward a motion,

53:08.320 --> 53:12.456
I believe the clerk's staff has wording already

53:12.528 --> 53:16.360
on that. So when I met

53:16.400 --> 53:20.024
you, do you want to move forward with that,

53:20.112 --> 53:23.402
or do you want the clerk staff to read the

53:23.426 --> 53:26.682
prepared motion? That they have, or we're going to pop it up on the screen

53:26.746 --> 53:29.338
for you guys to have a look at.

53:29.394 --> 53:32.666
Potentially. Pardon me. Sure.

53:32.738 --> 53:35.790
That I'm happy to move what's on the screen.

53:37.170 --> 53:41.658
Yeah. Just so everyone's clear, Councillor Erb was going to move a motion

53:41.754 --> 53:44.954
to take no action, but that is

53:45.122 --> 53:48.690
a motion that's not required.

53:49.990 --> 53:53.262
No motion, no action. Yes. So, Chantelle, that the

53:53.286 --> 53:57.134
regional municipality of Waterloo direct staff to prepare a bylaw to repeal the

53:57.302 --> 54:00.490
prohibition of election signs on.

54:01.750 --> 54:05.406
On regional roads and return to the rules for election signs

54:05.438 --> 54:09.450
that were in place in 2018. Thank you.

54:09.830 --> 54:13.534
Yeah. So, Councillor Vrbanovic, is this something along the lines

54:13.582 --> 54:16.418
as to. In your comments you are proposing. Prepared to move.

54:16.554 --> 54:20.354
Yes, I. Sorry, I guess you didn't hear me. I indicated I'm prepared to

54:20.362 --> 54:23.650
move the. The motion on the screen. Do we have a seconder for that,

54:23.690 --> 54:27.042
Councillor Wolf? I'll second. Okay. So we do have

54:27.066 --> 54:30.306
a continued speakers list. So if you don't mind,

54:30.378 --> 54:31.670
Councillor Shantz.

54:33.770 --> 54:37.330
Thank you. I'm. I'm opposed to the

54:37.370 --> 54:40.786
recommendation. I think there's lots of places people can

54:40.858 --> 54:44.686
put signs. And I

54:44.838 --> 54:48.622
constantly hear, even in my last election, well, so. And so is

54:48.646 --> 54:52.078
going to win in whatever riding because they've

54:52.094 --> 54:55.370
got more signs. And so I

54:55.830 --> 54:58.850
don't like that signs are becoming,

55:01.350 --> 55:05.422
I don't know, a de facto endorsement

55:05.486 --> 55:08.658
of candidates. If your sign

55:08.714 --> 55:11.030
is on an individual's lawn,

55:11.450 --> 55:15.458
that means someone is supporting you. And so even,

55:15.514 --> 55:18.882
even a new person. We've talked about going door to door and

55:18.906 --> 55:22.402
how important it is to go door to door. And if you're going door to

55:22.426 --> 55:25.826
door, you can ask people if you can put your sign

55:25.898 --> 55:29.190
up whether you're new or whether you're an incumbent.

55:30.090 --> 55:33.416
So I'm opposed to,

55:33.458 --> 55:36.940
to the motion. I think that there, there are

55:36.980 --> 55:40.924
lots of places that can be found to put signs

55:41.052 --> 55:44.812
if you want signs. I don't think regional roads are

55:44.996 --> 55:49.120
good places. Okay. Chair Redman,

55:50.180 --> 55:53.996
thank you. I would just concur with my colleague, Councillor Shantz.

55:54.108 --> 55:57.400
I think there's lots of places to put signs. I have to say that

55:58.100 --> 56:01.964
as a regional chair that runs region wide, I certainly

56:02.012 --> 56:05.528
have looked to places in fields and on road

56:05.584 --> 56:08.776
allowances throughout the region. My sign

56:08.848 --> 56:12.312
chair was really happy when this came forward, so we

56:12.336 --> 56:15.912
were able to use small signs on people's lawns. Having said

56:15.936 --> 56:19.032
that, I've still got all my signs. So if we change this, I can go

56:19.056 --> 56:22.216
back out and we will put them back up on regional road allowances.

56:22.328 --> 56:25.720
But there are many other places where you can put signs and for

56:25.760 --> 56:29.188
me and one of the delegations talked about this, this was about

56:29.244 --> 56:32.724
volunteer safety and it isn't safe to

56:32.732 --> 56:36.852
be on regional roads. They're usually busy. Often the

56:36.956 --> 56:40.932
speed limit is a bit higher than on residential roads.

56:40.996 --> 56:45.556
And for that reason I thought this was a matter of everybody's volunteer

56:45.748 --> 56:50.148
safety. All volunteers, not just my volunteers. So I

56:50.284 --> 56:53.764
like the prohibition on regional roads and I

56:53.772 --> 56:57.794
would just like to commend Councillor James for raising. I realize

56:57.842 --> 57:01.730
that that's a dated report, it was 2021,

57:01.770 --> 57:05.602
which is probably longer ago than any of us realize,

57:05.666 --> 57:09.990
but I too find that wording really problematic.

57:10.330 --> 57:13.186
Thank you. Okay.

57:13.378 --> 57:17.042
Lots of interest in this topic, Councillor Nowak. Yeah, I think I

57:17.066 --> 57:19.910
probably know the answer to this, but I'm going to ask it anyways.

57:20.300 --> 57:24.452
The rules around a motion to reconsider not apply

57:24.516 --> 57:28.324
to a new council. Yeah, that was actually a

57:28.332 --> 57:32.180
good question. That that motion was approved the last

57:32.300 --> 57:36.484
term of council. So this would be under fresh new pretenses.

57:36.532 --> 57:39.760
So it would be. And again, the, the report here,

57:41.260 --> 57:44.880
you know, would only ask staff to come back for

57:45.180 --> 57:49.082
a drafting of a bylaw. And then obviously there would be another opportunity for

57:49.106 --> 57:52.154
council to weigh in at that given time. So,

57:52.242 --> 57:55.590
you know, today if you were to vote and approve this

57:55.970 --> 57:59.354
request of staff, it would simply be that to bring back an official

57:59.442 --> 58:03.258
bylaw and then council would make a decision based on that

58:03.314 --> 58:07.150
recommendation. That's the answer to my second question. Thank you. Excellent.

58:07.890 --> 58:11.110
Councillor McCabe. Thank you.

58:13.020 --> 58:16.964
Yeah, part of where I guess I'm kind of puzzled

58:17.012 --> 58:20.804
a bit by the interest, the overwhelming

58:20.852 --> 58:23.908
interest in this is that really if we

58:23.964 --> 58:27.092
want to engage equity

58:27.156 --> 58:30.532
